---

<Base title="What Is EMDR Therapy? EMDR in Connecticut | Hopewell" description="What is EMDR therapy? A trauma therapy that helps people process trauma and PTSD, with a trained Hopewell clinician in Connecticut. See what sessions are like." canonical={canonical} image="/Assets/Blogs/recovering-from-narcissistic-abuse-when-therapy-alone-isnt-enough-hero.webp">
  <Header />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="hw">
    <ServiceHero
      crumbs={[{ label: "Treatments", href: "/treatment-methods/" }, { label: "EMDR" }]}
      title="What Is EMDR Therapy? EMDR in Connecticut"
      lede="An evidence-based, eight-phase trauma therapy that helps people heal from disturbing life experiences, offered by our clinicians across Connecticut."
      bullets={heroUsps}
      facts={[{ icon: "heart", text: "Trauma therapy" }, { icon: "users", text: "In person across Connecticut" }]}
      image="/Assets/Blogs/recovering-from-narcissistic-abuse-when-therapy-alone-isnt-enough-hero.webp"
    />
    <Creds />

    <section class="section is-white">
      <div class="container tx-read">
        <div class="tx-copy">
          <h2>What is EMDR therapy?</h2>
          <p style="margin-top:16px">EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing) is a psychotherapy that enables people to heal from the symptoms and emotional distress that are the result of disturbing life experiences. Repeated studies show that by using EMDR therapy people can experience the benefits of psychotherapy that once took years to make a difference. It is widely assumed that severe emotional pain requires a long time to heal. EMDR therapy shows that the mind can in fact heal from <a class="inl" href="/what-we-treat/trauma-ptsd/">psychological trauma</a> much as the body recovers from physical trauma.</p>
          <p>When you cut your hand, your body works to close the wound. If a foreign object or repeated injury irritates the wound, it festers and causes pain. Once the block is removed, healing resumes. EMDR therapy demonstrates that a similar sequence of events occurs with mental processes. The brain’s information processing system naturally moves toward mental health. If the system is blocked or imbalanced by the impact of a disturbing event, the emotional wound festers and can cause intense suffering. Once the block is removed, healing resumes. Using the detailed protocols and procedures learned in EMDR therapy training sessions, clinicians help clients activate their natural healing processes. Read <a class="inl" href="/post/emdr-therapy-what-it-is-side-effects-and-what-to-expect/">what to expect from EMDR, including side effects</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <aside>
          <img src="/Assets/Blogs/emdr-therapy-what-it-is-side-effects-and-what-to-expect.webp" alt="A therapist guiding a client's eye movements with two fingers during an EMDR session" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" />
          <blockquote class="tx-pull">“I survived it and I am strong.”<small>The kind of belief EMDR helps a person reach</small></blockquote>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who EMDR may help (brief): two-column list divided by a thin rule -->
    <section class="section is-stone">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2>Who EMDR may help</h2><p class="lede">EMDR, eye movement desensitization and reprocessing, uses guided eye movements or tapping while you recall a memory, so it feels less overwhelming.</p></div>
        <div class="emdr-cols">
          <div>
            <h3>Best known for</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><a class="inl" href="/what-we-treat/trauma-ptsd/">Trauma and PTSD</a>, the main use of EMDR</li>
              <li>Distressing memories that still feel overwhelming</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Sometimes used for</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><a class="inl" href="/what-we-treat/anxiety/">Anxiety</a></li>
              <li><a class="inl" href="/post/fear-of-flying-or-fear-of-leaving-home/">Phobias</a></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:22px;font-size:15.5px;color:var(--hw-slate)">EMDR is one of several approaches our clinicians use, alongside <a class="inl" href="/programs/counseling/">counseling</a> and <a class="inl" href="/treatment-methods/dbt/">DBT</a>. For more structured trauma support, see our <a class="inl" href="/programs/womens-trauma-iop/">Women’s Trauma IOP</a> and <a class="inl" href="/programs/first-responder-iop/">First Responder IOP</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section is-navy">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2>The research</h2></div>
        {/* Research graphic (user 2026-11-18): ring charts instead of plain stat cards. Ring fill = the study figure
            (84–90% drawn at the midpoint, 87%). */}
        <ul class="er-rings" aria-label="EMDR outcome studies">
          {stats.map(([n, u, d, pct, sess]) => (
            <li class="er-ring">
              <div class="er-dial">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 120 120" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="er-track" cx="60" cy="60" r="50" /><circle class="er-fill" cx="60" cy="60" r="50" pathLength="100" style={`stroke-dasharray:${pct} 100`} /></svg>
                <strong>{n}<em>{u}</em></strong>
              </div>
              <span class="er-sess">{sess}</span>
              <span class="er-d">{d}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        {/* Research facts (user 2026-11-18: "graphics, bullet points and interlinking"). Every fact from the live
            paragraph is kept: the three ring figures above, these four tiles, and the two bullets below. */}
        <ul class="er-facts" aria-label="EMDR research at a glance">
          <li><span class="er-fi"><Icon name="clipboard" class="h-6 w-6" /></span><strong>30+</strong><span>positive controlled outcome studies have been done on EMDR therapy</span></li>
          <li><span class="er-fi"><Icon name="shield" class="h-6 w-6" /></span><strong>APA · WHO · DoD</strong><span>recognize it as an effective treatment for <a class="inl" href="/what-we-treat/trauma-ptsd/">trauma</a> and other disturbing experiences: the American Psychiatric Association, the World Health Organization and the Department of Defense</span></li>
          <li><span class="er-fi"><Icon name="users" class="h-6 w-6" /></span><strong>100,000+</strong><span>clinicians throughout the world use the therapy</span></li>
          <li><span class="er-fi"><Icon name="heart" class="h-6 w-6" /></span><strong>Millions</strong><span>of people treated successfully over the past 25 years</span></li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="er-notes">
          <li><span class="er-ck"><Icon name="check" class="h-4 w-4" /></span><span>The same Kaiser Permanente (HMO) study also found that <b>77% of multiple-trauma victims</b> were no longer diagnosed with <a class="inl" href="/what-we-treat/trauma-ptsd/">PTSD</a> after only six 50-minute sessions.</span></li>
          <li><span class="er-ck"><Icon name="check" class="h-4 w-4" /></span><span>Given that worldwide recognition, EMDR can also help with the “everyday” memories behind <a class="inl" href="/what-we-treat/depression/">low self-esteem and feelings of powerlessness</a>, and the many other problems that bring people in for <a class="inl" href="/programs/counseling/">therapy</a>. For focused trauma care, see our <a class="inl" href="/programs/womens-trauma-iop/">Women’s Trauma IOP</a>.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="tx-source">Figures as reported in published EMDR outcome studies, including the Kaiser Permanente study cited above.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <style>
